# own, so this stands beside a version that has moved past it until one for
# 0.7.0 is reserved. The README says the same in prose.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22299353
keywords:
  - Keller maps
  - Jacobian conjecture
  - polynomial automorphisms
  - certified computation
  - computer algebra
  - SymPy
abstract: >-
  A Python library that constructs and verifies reductions of polynomial maps
  with constant Jacobian determinant. Every transformation is a certificate
  object that carries its own obligations, and verification names the
  obligation that failed rather than returning a boolean; a collision is
  transported across each step and re-verified, so a reduction of a
  counterexample is still a counterexample.

  Milestone 0.7 completes the Bass-Connell-Wright Reduction Theorem, including
  the multi-affine normal form of its part (b), and adds a descent that deletes
  a coordinate two determinant-one changes have made triangular. Milestone 0.6
  had implemented the second and third stages of that theorem, collision-hull
  compression, and the symmetric lift to the gradient form of a quartic. The
  constructions are other people's; what
  this project contributes is the composition of them, the certificates, and
  eight independent reconstruction scripts that recompute the published results
  without using the library. No minimality and no priority is claimed for any
  figure it reaches: docs/references.md states what each comparison does and
  does not establish, and docs/errata.md records what this project reported
  wrongly and corrected.

  Generative AI tools were used in writing this software and in auditing its
  release candidates. docs/provenance.md says which tools, in which roles, and
  what was verified by whom. Neither model is an author.
